WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:09.200
Oil prices have been weak for all of
2020, in part because of the shale energy

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:13.639
revolution in America, but also in part
to slow global growth. It's a problem

00:00:13.639 --> 00:00:17.540
we've had since late 2014. Since late
2016,

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:21.529
there was a mitigation strategy in place
by OPEC members, plus a few more key

00:00:21.529 --> 00:00:25.939
producers like Russia and Mexico.What's
new is that the COVID-19 crisis

00:00:25.939 --> 00:00:30.320
has disrupted Chinese economic activity,
and with it global supply chains and the

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:35.449
demand for transport fuel. What's also
new is that the 2016 strategy OPEC+

00:00:35.449 --> 00:00:39.949
used to cut production has fallen apart.
So we've got way too much oil on the

00:00:39.949 --> 00:00:44.180
market, which the Saudis are selling at a
discount. It's an oil flood. We're seeing

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:48.260
free-market movement, but it is
weaponized by state-owned oil. The Saudis

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:51.199
are trying to starve out shale
production and take market share from

00:00:51.199 --> 00:00:55.519
Russia, while hoping the Chinese economy
will pick up soon and COVID-19 doesn't

00:00:55.519 --> 00:00:59.960
put the global economy into recession.
The good news is we have plenty of oil.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:03.979
The bad news is we need the global
economy, especially the Chinese economy,

00:01:03.979 --> 00:01:06.540
to pick up demand.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:11.000
Do you think the COVID-19 crisis and declining oil prices are

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:15.080
related? let us know in our poll. Also, let
us know what other topics you'd like our

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:18.740
scholars to cover in 60 seconds, and be
sure to like and subscribe for more

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:22.480
research and videos from AEI.

